Razor doesn't correctly parse an escaped alias name in an `@using` directive. These are valid C# aliases, but Razor doesn't emit the alias declaration into the generated C#.

Found while investigating the review feedback on https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/pull/86243#discussion_r4406431152. This is a pre-existing compiler parsing limitation, separate from that PR's split-document rename fix.

### Repro

Put either of these examples in a `.razor` component.

An alias for a named type:

```razor
@using @class = System.String

@((@class)null)

@code {
    private @class? _value;
}
```

An alias for a tuple type:

```razor
@using @class = (int, int)

@(default(@class))

@code {
    private @class _value;
}
```

### Expected behaviour

Razor recognises the complete `@using` directive and emits the escaped alias into the generated C#, so its inline and member references bind to that alias.

### Actual behaviour

In the tuple repro, the generated implementation document contains an incomplete `using` inside `BuildRenderTree`, and ` = (int, int)` is emitted as markup. There is no alias declaration for the references to bind to. Both examples also return no rename edit when attempting to rename an existing `@class` alias, from either its declaration or an inline reference.

This was reproduced in shared cohost endpoint tests on the branch at `d02e7f3be877a6682c66d0e922e9e356f65ffd06`, before any escaped-identifier validation changes.

### Rename coverage when fixing this

Please include rename regression coverage alongside the parsing/code-generation tests. Fixing parsing alone isn't enough: the tooling's alias-identifier check currently uses `SyntaxFacts.IsValidIdentifier`, which rejects source spellings such as `@class`.

For example, start with:

```razor
@using Pair = (int, int)

@(default(Pair))

@code {
    private Pair _value;
}
```

Renaming `Pair` to `class` currently changes the references to `@class` but leaves `@using Pair = (int, int)` unchanged. The using-delta fallback excludes tuple aliases, so it doesn't recover the declaration edit.

Once escaped aliases parse correctly, cover renames both **to and from escaped names**, for named-type and tuple aliases. Verify the directive and all inline/member references are updated across both generated implementation and declaration documents. Include legacy `.cshtml` compatibility coverage too.
